  - never: promote ignored generated schema or a machine-specific executable path into portable semantic authority

& [SecurityBoundary] is explicit
  + [authentication] is owned by the local Codex installation; Cantor never reads, copies, logs, or serializes its credential
  + [process] is an operator-supplied canonical regular executable matched to expected SHA-256 and version output
  + [MCP] is an operator-supplied canonical cantor-mcp executable and exact environment matched to expected digests
  + [model] is not selected by Cantor Phase 2; the configured Codex default is used unless a later contract pins a discovered model
  + [output] is untrusted candidate text until decoded, correlated, core-verified, and Observer-reviewed
  - never: infer app-server access from API-key possession or bypass host approval, sandbox, policy, or managed requirements

& [Conclusion] is a bounded live checkpoint seam
  + [supported] is external manager to Codex thread and turn control plus Codex to Cantor MCP tool use with streamed inspectable events
  + [not_supported_claim] is shared token-level inference, hidden-state exchange, KV-cache mutation, asynchronous mid-token notification, or direct model-attention control
  + [implementation_candidate] is one ephemeral read-only turn that calls exactly one Cantor tool and returns one schema-bound candidate
  + [next_gate] is source-preserved SJS activation, deterministic fake-server protocol tests, then one opt-in live proof on the locally fingerprinted executable
